  "subtitle": "A whole recording schedule compressed into a printed number you type into a box",
  "why_scout_cares": "VCR Plus+ (Gemstar, $60 retail, November 1990) is one of the strangest and most successful physical-ritual interfaces of the era: TV listings printed a bold 4-to-8 digit 'PlusCode' next to every program, and you looked up the number in print, typed it into a small box, and pressed Once/Daily/Weekly — the box then fired the IR remote codes of your particular VCR at the scheduled moment to record the show. The printed digits were a compressed encoding of channel, date, start time and duration (the G-Code/PlusCode algorithm), so typing one number replaced the infamous 1980s chore of VCR programming menus. The algorithm was reverse-engineered and published in the journal Cryptologia in 1992 by Ken Shirriff. It sold in the tens of millions, grew into the ShowView and Japanese G-Code variants, and the company merged with TV Guide itself. The museum has barcodes (Cauzin Softstrip, TI Magic Wand, Barcode Battler) but nothing where the human reads printed decimal digits and types them as a machine command.",
